00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:55.000
How do you make sure that you have a data set on which you train your machine learning model?

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:03.000
From within that, you sample some part of the data set and take it out as your evaluation data set.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:13.000
Whereas if you want to evaluate an LLM based application, then you do not have this particular data set available.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
So what do we have to do?

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.000
We have to make this data set in some way.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.000
Now there were three methods to make this.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.000
There were three types or three methods you can say through which we can make this data set.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.000
What was the first method?

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:35.000
The first method was to generate the data set using humans.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:41.000
You can create a data set to evaluate an LLM based application with the help of humans.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:53.000
We also read that if we create a data set through humans, then it is time consuming, costly and scalable.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:02.000
If you want to do your evaluation again and again on a certain frequency, then evaluating through humans is not a scalable system.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:11.000
The second method we found is that whatever queries we have in the application,

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:17.000
because we are building a rag application, then our user will send a query to our application.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:23.000
So whatever queries are coming, we can lock it in some way, save it somewhere.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:32.000
And through these queries, we can test our LLM based application.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.000
Now what was the problem in this?

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:40.000
The problem was that we were getting queries in the production.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:47.000
But the problem was that the answers that we needed to get the correct answers to the queries were missing.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:52.000
So again to generate these answers, we had to use a human.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:59.000
And the second problem was that we deployed our application without properly evaluating it.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:03.000
So if there is a problem in the production, then we cannot solve it.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.000
We have to take down our application.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.000
So this was the second type in which you evaluate your production data.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.000
But for that you again need the human's requirement.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:22.000
The third method that we had studied was our generating of data set using LLMs.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.000
We will generate the data set using LLMs.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.000
So what we had understood in this is that we will give our context to our LLMs.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:37.000
Meaning that we will give our knowledge base to our LLMs.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:46.000
And our LLM will generate this data set through a pipeline or through a workflow.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:53.000
Meaning that whatever documents are read in the knowledge base, we will get this data set on their basis.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:01.000
So here the third method was that we finalized it as a data set creation method.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.000
What can we do with this help?

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.000
With this help we can generate the data set very fast.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.000
The second benefit is that it is scalable.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:17.000
And the third benefit is that it costs less than your humans.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.000
So the cost is an advantage.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:31.000
And we can use this type of data set generation in different iterations.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:35.000
So this gives us reproducibility.

