00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:36.000
So as we have already discussed, what is our issue here?

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:46.000
Our issue is that we have multiple documents in our knowledge base.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:53.000
Whenever you build a rag application, do you build it on a single knowledge base?

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.000
The answer is no.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:59.000
You have multiple documents on which you build your rag application.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:07.000
And in our case also, we are building a rag chatbot for campusX.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:19.000
And this chatbot will assist our students in solving doubts.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:33.000
Basically, we have added all the transcripts of our live classes to our knowledge base.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:41.000
And here we have made a rag chatbot which retrieves the documents from this knowledge base.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:48.000
And it generates answers on this retrieved context.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.000
Is this clear?

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:59.000
Now our main concern of generating our evaluation is that whatever evaluation we have to do for rag chatbot,

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:05.000
it should be our context-based or grounded.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:17.000
This means that the questions we have to generate should be solely from our knowledge base.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.000
It should not be outside our knowledge base.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:29.000
As we have covered in the example that if you want to make a paper, then this paper should be inside your ncrt.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:32.000
You cannot ask questions outside ncrt.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:45.000
So the mechanism we adopted was that we will use deep eval synthesizer.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:54.000
And this deep eval synthesizer will take my context or all my knowledge.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:14:06.000
And on the basis of this knowledge, it will generate my evaluation data set or my questions.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:12.000
But we also read that there are not only questions in our data set.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:18.000
If you have questions in any question paper, then you cannot evaluate it properly.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:22.000
You should have answer key along with questions.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:31.000
It is obvious that if you have only questions and your students have written answers, then how will you evaluate it?

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:38.000
You should have a criteria, a ground on which you can compare and evaluate.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:49.000
Let's say that answer key has answer A and student has written answer B which is similar to answer A.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:54.000
So if this is the case, then I will score this student B as high.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:58.000
Let's say that I have to score it from 10, then I will score it as 8 or 9.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:02.000
Because its answer is similar to my answer key.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:08.000
Whereas if there is another student who has written answer C, then it is not similar to my answer.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.000
So what I will do is that I will score it low in this case.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.000
Let's say that I will score it 2 or 3.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.000
And who is doing all this?

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.000
All this is done by LLM.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:23.000
And LLMs are good at understanding your language patterns.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:28.000
So here the exact keyword matching is not happening from inside my answer during the evaluation.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Here basically intent matching is happening.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:39.000
So always remember that you don't do exact keyword to keyword matching.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:47.000
It is not necessary that the answer B which has got a high score, the words written in it are similar to answer A.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:50.000
That is not the primary requirement.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:57.000
The requirement is that the facts written by the student or application in answer B,

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:02.000
the intent of the answer should match with the key.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:07.000
That is why we use LLMs here because LLMs are good at intent matching.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:12.000
We basically calculate similarities here.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:15.000
It should not be the exact.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.000
Is this clear to everyone?

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:29.000
Now let's discuss how this whole workflow works.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:43.000
So what we have to do here is that we have to generate questions from our context.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:52.000
And we have one more limitation that the questions we have to generate should be limited in number.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:57.000
They cannot be infinite because we are generating questions through LLM.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:05.000
And you know that whenever you use LLMs, the cost of LLMs is the API.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:08.000
You have to bear the cost of all the API calls.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:12.000
So it is not that you can generate infinite questions here.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:14.000
There has to be a limit.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:19.000
So what we have to do here is that we have to decide how many questions we have to generate.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:22.000
So let's say I am the examiner.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:29.000
I am doing this evaluation and I have decided that I need 30 questions in my exam.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:37.000
And the second problem is that the context is very vast.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:41.000
So I have to do a smart approach here.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.000
What does smart approach mean?

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:52.000
First of all, I have to filter out the most useful context from my context.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:57.000
It is very obvious that I have to create 30 questions here.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.000
I have a limited number of questions here.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:01.000
But the context is very big.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:05.000
So I have to assemble my context properly.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:10.000
I can create questions from a limited set of context.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:11.000
Is this thing clear?

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:16.000
So the first thing is that I have to create this context and assemble this context.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:22.000
And secondly, what I have to do is that I have to make questions from the filtered context.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:26.000
Those should also be of good quality.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:29.000
I can't make light questions.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:33.000
Because what I have to do here is that I have to evaluate my student.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.000
I have to evaluate my application.

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:40.000
So the questions I am making should also be of good quality.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:44.000
Again, I have to do one more set of filtration here.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:48.000
I hope you are understanding this point.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:52.000
So what I mean to say is that you have to do two levels of filtration here.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:56.000
First of all, you have to filter your context here.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:04.000
Because you make questions from the context of your BRAC-based evaluation.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:08.000
You set your question paper from the context.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.000
So this is your first prerequisite.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:15.000
So what we have to do here is that we have to refine this context.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:22.000
From where we are making questions, our source should be the best source.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:23.000
Is this thing clear?

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:27.000
And secondly, the questions we are generating from this context,

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:31.000
we will not select only the questions from the first pass.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:37.000
We will filter those questions further.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:45.000
So that the questions we have in the end and the answers related to them are our best of the best.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:50.000
That's why they are called Golden or Golden dataset.

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:53.000
Basically, what is the golden dataset?

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.000
Golden dataset is a template of you, a blueprint.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.000
Blueprint of what?

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:02.000
Blueprint of the best of the best questions.

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:08.000
Based on this, you will evaluate your LLM-based application.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:12.000
And there should be two or three things in this blueprint.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:18.000
First of all, the questions you will generate should not be straightforward questions.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.000
They should not be very simple questions.

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:22.000
It should be a little tricky.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:27.000
That's why when we generate these questions, we evolve them further.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:28.000
We make them better.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:33.000
Second, you have to take care of coverage.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:40.000
Let's say, in your context, in your knowledge base, there are 50 documents.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:43.000
And you have to make only 30 questions here.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:48.000
So on an average, you are not making a question from one document.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:54.000
So what you have to do here is, first of all, you have to filter some of the best documents from these 50.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:57.000
Let's say, those are our 15 documents.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:00.000
These 15 documents are the best of all.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:04.000
Based on which we can evaluate our LLM-based application.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:11.000
And after that, you have to make sure that you generate two questions from each document.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:14.000
So what will happen with this?

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:16.000
You can also maintain coverage here.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.000
What does coverage mean?

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:24.000
You can test your LLM-based application from an all-rounded perspective.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:32.000
And third, you have to make this whole system scalable.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.000
It's not like you have generated these questions here.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:38.000
And it took you a lot of time to generate these questions.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:39.000
Or it took a lot of cost.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:43.000
So you have to make this whole thing scalable.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:48.000
And you have to basically generate this golden data set only once or twice.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:54.000
So you make it your source of truth or ground truth and save it.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:58.000
And you reuse it again and again.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:03.000
So what we generally do is, whenever we make an LLM-based application, like our RAG application.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:09.000
Now what we did in this RAG application is, we made our golden data set here.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:12.000
Which we are going to make in this lecture today.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:16.000
As soon as we create this golden data set, what we will do after that?

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:22.000
In upcoming lectures, we will make some changes in this RAG application.

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.000
We will make some iterations.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.000
We will change the value of some parameters.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:28.000
We can change some parameters in architecture.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.000
And what will happen with that?

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.000
Our entire pipeline will change.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:38.000
If the pipeline changes, then its performance may also change.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:41.000
It is not necessary that every time you change something, the performance changes.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:45.000
But if you are making major changes, then performance can change.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:49.000
So whatever changes you are making, they are called your experiments.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:57.000
So the experiments you will do, you will create this golden data set every time.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:59.000
You will not create it again and again.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:05.000
You will create it once and you will run this golden data set on these experiments every time.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:07.000
And evaluate your RAG pipeline.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:11.000
And you will calculate deltas here.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:13.000
